# Larkfield: the market town of the Larkdowns, rolling hills and open fields in the south-east, where the carters and
# caravans come in. The Singing Lark inn, the general store, the smithy, the market, the gate and its toll, the old
# well, the wagons, the beasts and the road. (The downs, the sheep and the town's own customs: larkfield_downs.scene.)

scene rg_ac_counting_wagons
when topic=smalltalk|lore here.region=larkfield
a : Forty-one wagons through since dawn. I counted. | Every wagon in the south creaks past my window. | The road's quiet today. Too quiet for market week.
b[carter] : Forty-one, and every one of them stopped for a drink.
b[old] : I've counted wagons here sixty years. The number never matters.
b : Busy day. | Good for trade. | Weather on the downs, maybe.
a : Very busy. | For some. | Maybe.

scene rg_ac_toll_grumble
when topic=smalltalk|work here.region=larkfield
a : The gate toll went up a penny a wheel. | A Ridgemere carter tried to argue the toll down. Lost. | Half the town lives off the market, the other half complains about the toll.
b[guard] : The town sets the toll. I just take it.
b[carter] : A penny a wheel, four wheels, every trip. It adds up.
b : Of course it did. | They always try. | And pay it anyway.
a : Of course. | Always. | Every time.

scene rg_ac_crossed_roads
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.place=tavern|street
a : The Singing Lark had carters sleeping on the tables last night. | Best stew in the south at the Singing Lark, they say. | Somebody started a song at the inn that lasted till dawn.
b[innkeeper] : Tables cost the same as beds, if you ask me. Nobody asks.
b[carter] : Best stew, worst beds. Fair trade.
b : Busy season. | They say that everywhere. | I heard it.
a : Very busy. | They do. | So did everyone.

scene rg_ac_carters_table
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : The carters' table by the fire is sacred. Sit there and you'll learn. | Only carters sit at the fire table at the inn. | A merchant sat at the carters' table once. Once.
b[carter] : Earn the seat, then take it. That's the rule.
b[innkeeper] : I don't make the rules. I just keep the fire.
b : Learned that the hard way. | Strange custom. | What happened?
a : We all do. | Old custom. | He stood after.

scene rg_ac_road_news
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : All the south's news comes through Larkfield market before it reaches anywhere else. | Heard from a carter what the Council of Houses decided, before the Houses had finished arguing. | News is cheaper than ale here.
b[merchant] : News is worth more than ale, if you know what to do with it.
b[old] : News at market's half true and half late. Same as anywhere.
b : That's market towns. | Carters talk. | And worth less.
a : It is. | Endlessly. | Usually.

scene rg_ac_gate_forge
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=smith|apprentice|carter
a : Three wheel rims and a dozen shoes before noon. | The smithy hasn't been cold in a month. | Every carter wants their rim done first.
b[smith] : Rims first, shoes after. A lame beast is slower than a bent wheel. Barely.
b : Hard morning. | Good trade. | They always do.
a : Hot one. | Hot trade. | Always.

scene rg_ac_beasts
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=herder|laborer|carter
a : The carters' beasts ate a whole cart of hay overnight. | One of the draught horses kicked the stable door off. | Beasts are nervous. Storm coming over the downs.
b[herder] : Feed them, water them, keep out from behind them.
b : Hungry beasts. | Mind your shins. | They always know.
a : Very hungry. | Always. | They do.

scene rg_ac_caravan_shed
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : The carters' shed's stacked to the roof waiting for the Ridgemere wagons. | Somebody's bales have sat in the shed since spring. | A crate in the shed's been ticking. Nobody'll open it.
b[merchant] : Unclaimed goods go to auction after a season. I've an eye on a few.
b[laborer] : I stack them, I don't ask what's in them.
b : Wagons are late. | Forgotten, maybe. | Ticking?
a : Very late. | Probably. | Ticking.

scene rg_ac_coins_jar
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : The innkeeper keeps a jar of coins from places nobody's heard of. | Got paid in a coin with a fish on it. Where's that from? | Every coin in the south passes through Larkfield market, sooner or later.
b[merchant] : A fish? Ridgemere wharf, I'd guess. Weigh it.
b[old] : I had a coin once from over the sea. Lost it in a wager.
b : Strange collection. | No idea. | Every one, they say.
a : Strange and heavy. | Nor me. | Nearly.

scene rg_ac_fields
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=farmer|laborer
a : The fields by the road are trampled again by stray beasts. | Wheel ruts right through my barley. | Hard to farm with the road at your door.
b[farmer] : Fence it. Fence it twice. They'll still get in.
b : Again? | Somebody should pay. | It is.
a : Again. | Nobody will. | Very hard.

scene rg_ac_lowland_ways
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : Three roads leave Larkfield, and every one goes somewhere different. | The north road goes to the city, the south to the grain fields. | You can be anywhere in a week from here.
b[carter] : And every one of them has a ditch I've slid into.
b[adolescent] : One day I'll take all three.
b : That's why it's here. | And the west to the fens. | Anywhere but home.
a : It is. | And the fens. | Anywhere.

scene rg_ac_toll_post
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.role=guard
a : A carter hid three barrels under his hay. | Toll book's short a penny and the sergeant wants to know why. | Spent the morning arguing with a Ridgemere factor.
b[guard] : Hay hides everything. Check the hay.
b : Clever. | Always is. | They always argue.
a : Not clever enough. | Always. | Always.

scene rg_ac_waystation_stores
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.place=street|market|work
a : The general store's out of rope again. | Hard bread at the store, hard enough to build with. | The store charges double on market day.
b[merchant] : Out of rope, out of luck. The next wagon brings more.
b : The carters bought it all. | It keeps, though. | Because they can.
a : All of it. | It keeps. | They can.

scene rg_ac_lost_find
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : Somebody found a strongbox in the ditch by the mile stone. | Nobody's come back for the cloak left at the inn. Two seasons. | The road gives up strange things after rain.
b[guard] : Bring it to the gate. We'll keep it.
b[old] : Never keep what the road leaves. Bad luck follows.
b : Full? | Somebody's missing it. | Bones, once, they say.
a : Empty. | Not anymore. | Bones.

scene rg_ac_accord_watch_cousin
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield pair.band=friends|family|acquaintances
a : My cousin in the Ridgemere Watch hasn't written since spring. | They say the city's busy with its Houses. Nobody comes south. | Wish I'd gone to the city when I was young.
b[old] : Letters come by the carters. Ask at the inn.
b : Busy, I suppose. | Their loss. | You still could.
a : I suppose. | Probably. | Maybe.

scene rg_ac_dust
when topic=smalltalk|weather here.region=larkfield here.season=summer
a : Road dust in my teeth again. | Every wagon raises a cloud. | The whole town's the colour of the road.
b[child] : I drew a wolf in the dust on the inn wall!
b[old] : Dust in summer, mud in autumn. Larkfield has two seasons.
b : That's summer. | Every one. | Grey as a toll book.
a : That's summer. | Every one. | Grey.

scene rg_ac_ruts
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.season=autumn|spring
a : The ruts in the square are knee deep. | Mud's swallowing wheels at the gate. | The town promised to lay stones in the square. Years ago.
b[carter] : Carts dug those ruts. Carts'll fill them, eventually.
b : Every season. | Every rain. | Promises, promises.
a : Every one. | Every rain. | Always promises.

scene rg_ac_stablehands
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield a.stage=young|adolescent
a : I groomed a carter's mare and he gave me a penny! | The big grey horse likes me. He nuzzled me. | When I grow up I'll drive my own wagon.
b[carter] : Penny well earned, pup. Do it again tomorrow.
b : Good work. | He's a gentle one. | You will.
a : I will. | He is. | I will!

scene rg_ac_inn_rooms
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.place=tavern
a : Every room in the Singing Lark is taken. Sleeping in the hayloft. | The rooms under the roof are cold as a well. | Only one shutter in my room, and it bangs.
b[innkeeper] : Hayloft's free and warm. Don't smoke.
b : Busy season. | They always are. | Wedge it.
a : Very busy. | Always. | I tried.

scene rg_ac_waystation_pride
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : Larkfield market's older than half the towns it feeds. | My grandmother says Larkfield was one well and a tree once. | Without Larkfield market, the city would go hungry.
b[old] : One well, one tree, one wolf selling water. That's how it started.
b : Older than it looks. | Before the road. | They'd never admit it.
a : Much older. | Long before. | Never.

scene rg_ac_carters_tales
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : A carter swore a wagon passed him on the downs with nobody driving it. | Carters' tales get taller every mile. | One carter says she drove the Ridgemere road fifty times and never once saw the sun.
b[carter] : Every word's true. Some of them twice.
b : Tall tale. | Every mile. | I believe that one.
a : Very tall. | Every one. | Me too.

scene rg_ac_toll_house_store
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.place=market|street
a : The market has everything, at twice the price. | Bought a lamp at market. It leaks. | The stallholders at market hear everything.
b[merchant] : A little of everything at a little more. That's honest trade.
b : That's Larkfield. | Take it back. | Everything and more.
a : It is. | I will. | Much more.

scene rg_ac_bandit_fear
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.time=dusk|night
a : Nobody leaves Larkfield after dark. | The carters bar the shed doors at night. | Lanterns on the road at night are either fools or bandits.
b[guard] : We watch the road. But it's a long road.
b : Wise. | Sensible. | Or both.
a : Very wise. | Very. | Both.

scene rg_ac_wagons_wheels
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=crafter|smith|carter
a : A whole wheel shattered on the mile stone. | The wheelwright's backed up a week. | Felloes and spokes, felloes and spokes. All day.
b[crafter] : Ash for spokes, elm for hubs. Nothing else lasts.
b : Bad luck. | Busy wheelwright. | Steady work.
a : Very bad. | Very busy. | Steady.

scene rg_ac_well
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : The old well's still the best water on the road. | Carters fill every barrel at our well. | They say the well's never run dry. Not once.
b[old] : That well's why Larkfield is here at all.
b : Sweet water. | They do. | Not once.
a : The sweetest. | Every one. | Not once.

scene rg_ac_far_places
when topic=smalltalk here.region=larkfield a.stage=adolescent b.stage=adult|old
a : Have you ever been up to Frosthollow? | What's Ridgemere like? Really like? | Is it true there's a sea past the city?
b[carter] : Been everywhere twice. It's all roads in the end.
b : Once. Too cold. | Wet. Very wet. | Grey and endless.
a : I'd still go.

scene rg_ac_rain_mountain
when topic=weather|smalltalk here.region=larkfield here.weather=rain|overcast|storm
a : Rain on the downs means mud on every road. | Carters won't set out in this. | Every wagon's staying put tonight.
b[carter] : Mud to the axles on the city road. I'm not mad.
b[innkeeper] : Full house again tonight, then.
b : True enough. | Nor would I. | Good for the inn.
a : Always. | Nor me. | Very good.

scene rg_ac_mule_train
when topic=smalltalk|work here.region=larkfield
a : A mule train came in over the downs. No wagon can get through the mud. | Mules are smarter than carters. Ask any carter. | The mules refused the hill road this morning.
b[herder] : A mule knows its own load. Never argue.
b : Mud out there. | Ha. | Smart mules.
a : Plenty of it. | Every carter says so. | Very smart.
